The mechanical stability of retained austenite can be affect by its chemical composition [ 36 , 37 ], size [ 38 , 39 ] and morphology [ 40 , 41 ]. In this study, the same austenitizing temperature and time could ensure identical composition and grain size of initial austenite before quenching. According to our previous study [ 42 ], traditional QT treatment is usually produce blocky retained austenite in microstructures. However, the martensite pre-quenching and subsequent bainite transformation is prone to produce film-like retained austenite and carbon enriched M/A islands. The TEM images in  Fig. 7 
                          show the typical morphology of film-like retained austenite in specimen heat-treated by martensite pre-quenching at 200 °C and bainite transformation at 240 °C. During martensite pre-quenching and subsequent bainite transformation process, retained austenite is prone to leave between pre-quenched martensite and bainite which is usually appear as film-like. Moreover, the average carbon contents in retained austenite is calculate base on XRD patterns ( Fig. 8 
                         ) in QT specimen and martensite pre-quenching specimen at 200 °C is 0.739% and 0.797%, respectively. The fraction of pre-quenched martensite increasing with martensite pre-quenching temperature decrease, it will increase the carbon content in the remaining austenite during subsequent bainite transformation due to carbon partitioning [ 43 , 44 ], which requires further driving force for the commencement of martensite transformation during oil quenching and leaves more carbon enriched M/A islands in room microstructures. Therefore, both film-like morphology and more carbon content in retained austenite is the main reason for its higher mechanical stability in martensite pre-quenching specimen than that of QT specimen.
